SETJMP(3)                      P32W Unix                       SETJMP(3)

NAME
       setjmp, longjmp - non-local goto

SYNOPSIS
       #include <setjmp.h>

       int setjmp(jmp_buf env);
       void longjmp(jmp_buf env, int val);

DESCRIPTION
       setjmp saves its caller's place in env and returns 0.
       longjmp(env, val) then returns to that place: setjmp returns
       a second time, now with val (or 1 if val is 0). It is the
       way out of deep calls on an error, or from a signal handler
       back to a command loop.

       The function that called setjmp must not have returned
       before longjmp is called.

       env (an array of 10 ints) holds the callee-saved registers
       r8 to r14 (r14 is the frame pointer), the stack pointer and
       the return address.

RETURN VALUE
       0 when called; val (never 0) when returning through longjmp.

EXAMPLES
       jmp_buf top;

       void onintr(int sig)
       {
           signal(SIGINT, onintr);     /* (caught signals reset) */
           longjmp(top, 1);
       }
       ...
       setjmp(top);                    /* back here on ^C */
       signal(SIGINT, onintr);
       for (;;) {
           ... read and run a command ...
       }

SEE ALSO
       signal(2)

BUGS
       cc keeps up to four often-used scalar locals or parameters of
       a function in registers r10 to r13. longjmp puts those back
       as they were when setjmp was called, so such a variable
       changed between setjmp and longjmp has its old value
       afterwards. Declare a local that changes after setjmp
       volatile (as C requires): cc keeps volatile locals in memory.
       (A volatile parameter is not kept out of a register; copy it
       to a volatile local.)

       There is no sigsetjmp or _setjmp (there are no signal masks
       to save).
